<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Jin (Korean state)</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 100 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A shadowy early polity of southern Korea, named in Chinese records as the predecessor of the three Han confederacies of Mahan, Jinhan and Byeonhan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Known only from brief Chinese mentions, Jin seems to have emerged in the southern half of the peninsula by about the 3rd century BCE, when it reportedly sought contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han China</a> but was blocked by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gojoseon">Gojoseon</a>. Its people belonged to the bronze-using culture of slender Korean bronze daggers and mirrors.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nothing certain is known of its government, which may have been a loose grouping of chiefdoms rather than a unified kingdom. Rich burials with bronze daggers, mirrors and bells suggest ritual leaders, and Chinese sources say that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jun-of-gojoseon">King Jun of Gojoseon</a> fled south to this region around 194 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the 1st century BCE the area had resolved into the Samhan confederacies of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mahan">Mahan</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jinhan">Jinhan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/byeonhan">Byeonhan</a>, which Chinese texts describe as successors of Jin. Whether Jin was ever a single state remains debated.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jin-korean-state.jpg" alt="Jin (Korean state)" width="320" height="501"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jin_State_Korea.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 100 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jin state, Chin, Jinguk</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-200&amp;focus=jin-korean-state" aria-label="Jin (Korean state) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7899 878 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jin (Korean state) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8115 1035h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8219 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8219 1134h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day South Korea. <a href="/atlas?year=-200&amp;focus=jin-korean-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
